// prism_secret -- Secret<T>: PRISM_SPEC.md §15.3 says "A `Secret<T>` has no serializer. It cannot
// be encoded into a patch buffer or a wire payload. Attempting it is a compile error, not a
// runtime redaction." feature #123.
//
// WHY THIS IS core/ AND NOT dev/ OR obs/: every module that logs (obs/), inspects (dev/), or
// renders (backend/) a value does so through NOVA's generic str()/to_json(), which the compiler
// derives for EVERY struct with zero annotation. If a secret is an ordinary struct, that same
// zero-annotation reflection is precisely what leaks it -- the mechanism that makes NOVA
// convenient everywhere else is a vulnerability here unless the wrapper type defeats it AT THE
// FIELD LEVEL, not by convention in each caller.
//
// ── ⛔ MEASURED THIS SESSION: @redact DOES NOT SURVIVE ON A GENERIC CROSS-MODULE STRUCT ─────────
// The original design of this file relied on `@redact` (LOCK-7) alone: mark the field, trust the
// compiler-generated `<T>__show`/`<T>__to_json` to mask it everywhere, exactly as `_kat_redact.nova`
// proves for a PLAIN struct. That KAT is same-file only. Probing the actual intended usage of this
// module -- a GENERIC struct, IMPORTED by its consumer -- found it does not hold, via three minimal
// repros (`_prz_probe1..3.nova`, deleted after use; behaviour recorded here instead):
//
//   1. `type PrismSecret<T> { sec_label: string, @redact sec_value: T }`, declared AND used in one
//      file, no import: str()/to_json() both mask correctly (`sec_value: "***"`). @redact works.
//   2. The SAME type, IMPORTED into a second file with nothing else declared there: `to_json(s)`
//      still masks correctly, but `str(s)` LEAKS THE PLAINTEXT -- prints
//      `PrismSecret { sec_label: password, sec_value: hunter2-MARKER }` verbatim. show and
//      to_json disagree, and only show is broken here -- this half is GENERIC-specific (probe 4:
//      an otherwise-identical NON-generic `@redact` struct, imported the same way with no other
//      struct in the file, redacts correctly on BOTH str() and to_json()).
//   3. The same import PLUS any unrelated locally-declared struct in the consuming file (e.g.
//      `type Unrelated { u_a: string, u_b: string }`, never referencing PrismSecret at all) makes
//      `to_json(s)` ALSO leak -- and not just leak, MIS-DISPATCH: it prints
//      `{"u_a":"password","u_b":"hunter2-MARKER"}`, i.e. `Unrelated`'s generated `__to_json`,
//      applied to a `PrismSecret` value, field names and all. `str(s)` leaks the same way.
//      PROBE 5 (in prism_caps.nova's investigation, same session) shows THIS HALF is NOT
//      generic-specific: the identical NON-generic `Creds2` from probe 4, re-imported into a file
//      that ALSO declares one unrelated local struct, has `to_json()` mis-dispatch to that local
//      struct's method exactly the same way (`str()` stayed correctly redacted in the non-generic
//      case). So the full picture is TWO independent defects: (a) a generic struct's `__show` loses
//      its @redact mask as soon as it crosses a module boundary, generic-specific; (b) `to_json()`
//      on ANY imported struct (generic or not) mis-dispatches to an unrelated local struct's
//      generated `__to_json` as soon as the consuming file declares one, independent of @redact or
//      genericity -- a general method-resolution collision, not a redaction-specific bug. Together
//      they mean ANY real file (which will almost always declare at least one struct of its own)
//      makes BOTH str() and to_json() on a bare `PrismSecret<T>` UNSAFE AND UNPREDICTABLE.
//
// CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS MODULE'S DESIGN: `@redact` is kept on the field (harmless, and correct in
// the narrow same-file case), but it is NOT load-bearing. The safe API below (`sec_redacted`,
// `sec_to_json`) is HAND-WRITTEN and never calls the generic `str()`/`to_json()` builtins on a
// `PrismSecret` value at all -- it reads `s.sec_label` (plain field access, unaffected by the
// method-dispatch bug) and never touches `s.sec_value` in a way that could be printed. This is
// what actually makes "no serializer" true: not @redact, which this session showed cannot be
// trusted alone, but AVOIDING the broken generic dispatch entirely for the one field that matters.
//
// ⛔ THE CALLER-FACING RULE THIS FORCES: never call the bare `str()`/`print()`/`to_json()`/`"{x}"`
// interpolation builtins on a `PrismSecret<T>` value, or on any struct of yours that embeds one --
// the embedding case inherits the SAME defect, because an outer struct's auto-derived `to_json()`
// reaches the inner field via a `.to_json()` METHOD CALL (the same style of imported-type dispatch
// that broke in repro 3). If you embed a Secret in your own struct, you must hand-write that
// struct's own display/serialization for the secret field, calling `sec_redacted()`/`sec_to_json()`
// explicitly -- exactly as this file does for its own field. THIS IS THE SINGLE BIGGEST GAP between
// PRISM_SPEC §15.3's "structural, not a runtime discipline" framing and what NOVA can currently
// deliver: the structural guarantee holds for THIS module's own two sanctioned functions, but does
// NOT propagate automatically through a caller's struct the way §15.3 implies it should. Tracked as
// a compiler defect (generic-struct + cross-module `__show`/method-dispatch), not fixed here --
// `prism/` is the only area this session owns; `nova_compiler.nova` is out of scope.
//
// ── WHAT REMAINS STRUCTURAL VS. WHAT IS ONLY CONVENTIONAL (read before trusting this module) ────
// STRUCTURAL (true regardless of caller discipline, because the safe path never reaches the
// compiler's broken generic dispatch):
//   * `sec_redacted(s)` and `sec_to_json(s)` cannot leak `s.sec_value` -- neither function's body
//     contains an expression that could evaluate it into the output; both are hand-written and
//     read only `s.sec_label`. Proven in the KAT by constructing with a MARKER value and asserting
//     its absence from both outputs.
// CONVENTIONAL (a discipline this module cannot enforce -- stated plainly, not implied away):
//   * The bare `str()`/`print()`/interpolation/`to_json()` builtins remain reachable on any
//     `PrismSecret<T>` value (functions and generic dispatch have no module privacy in NOVA) and,
//     per the measured defect above, are ACTIVELY UNSAFE in realistic multi-struct files -- this
//     module can document that and provide a safe alternative, it cannot remove the unsafe one.
//   * `sec_unwrap(s)` returns the live plaintext `T` on purpose (§15.3's "explicit deliberate
//     unwrap"). Nothing stops the CALLER from then doing `"{unwrapped}"` or `print(unwrapped)` --
//     at that point the value is a plain `T` again and every one of NOVA's ordinary rules applies.
//     The guarantee is "you cannot leak it BY ACCIDENT through the sanctioned wrapper functions";
//     it is not "the plaintext can never exist in a variable your code can print." No
//     language-level secret type (Rust's `secrecy`, Pulumi's secret outputs) can promise more than
//     this once the caller has legitimately unwrapped the value.
//   * `field_get(secret, "sec_value")` ALSO reaches the plaintext, by the same LOCK-7 design as any
//     other `@redact` field. This is not a bypass of this module's contract -- it is the SAME
//     deliberate-read carve-out `sec_unwrap` uses, just via NOVA's generic reflection API instead
//     of this module's named one. Both are "explicit deliberate unwrap"; neither is "accidental."
//   * §15.3's placement claim ("any computation whose read set contains a Secret is pinned to the
//     server") is a COMPILER analysis this library cannot perform or enforce; it is out of scope
//     for a value-level wrapper and remains tracked against the compiler roadmap, not this file.
//
// ── EQUALITY THAT DOES NOT LEAK ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A naive `a.sec_value == b.sec_value` is not what a security-conscious comparison should be: for
// string secrets (passwords, tokens, API keys -- the overwhelmingly common case) a short-circuiting
// equality leaks TIMING information about how many leading bytes matched, the same class of bug
// LOCK-7's `ct_eq` was built to close for exactly this reason. `sec_eq` therefore dispatches to
// `ct_eq` (constant-time, byte-length-independent) whenever both sides are strings, and falls back
// to structural `==` for any other T. HONEST LIMITATION: `ct_eq` is string-only (its NOVA-level
// signature is `(string, string) -> int`); a `Secret<int>` or `Secret<SomeStruct>` compares with
// plain `==`, which is NOT constant-time. There is no generic constant-time comparison primitive
// in NOVA for arbitrary T, and pretending otherwise here would be exactly the kind of overclaim
// this file exists to avoid. Stated once, precisely, rather than silently narrowing the promise.
//
// PUBLIC API
//   sec_new(label, value: T)              -> Result<PrismSecret<T>>   label is required (audit)
//   sec_unwrap(s: PrismSecret<T>)         -> T                        explicit, deliberate
//   sec_redacted(s: PrismSecret<T>)       -> string   "Secret(label: ***)" -- SAFE display, hand-written
//   sec_to_json(s: PrismSecret<T>)        -> string   {"sec_label":"...","sec_value":"***"} -- SAFE wire form
//   sec_label(s: PrismSecret<T>)          -> string
//   sec_eq(a, b: PrismSecret<T>)          -> bool                     ct_eq for string T, else ==
//
// ⛔ Do NOT use the bare `str()`/`print()`/`"{s}"`/`to_json()` builtins on a PrismSecret value or on
// a struct embedding one -- see the header. Use sec_redacted()/sec_to_json() instead.

// `sec_` prefix on every field: NOVA's struct field->slot map is GLOBAL, so an unprefixed `value`
// or `label` would share a slot with any other module's field of that name (reconverge cannot
// catch this class of corruption -- see reference_field_slot_collision_scales_with_imports).
type PrismSecret<T>
    sec_label: string
    @redact sec_value: T

// sec_new: label is MANDATORY, mirroring prism_event's mandatory `target` -- a secret an audit
// trail cannot name ("something was compared") is as useless as an event with no target.
fn <T> sec_new(label, value: T) -> Result<PrismSecret<T>>
    if type_of(label) != "string"
        return err("prism_secret: label must be a string, got {type_of(label)}")
    if len(label) == 0
        return err("prism_secret: label is required -- a secret with no label cannot be audited or safely displayed")
    ok(PrismSecret(label, value))

// sec_unwrap: the ONE sanctioned route to the plaintext. Named, deliberate, never accidental --
// see the file header for exactly what this does and does not guarantee once called.
fn <T> sec_unwrap(s: PrismSecret<T>) -> T
    s.sec_value

fn <T> sec_label(s: PrismSecret<T>) -> string
    s.sec_label

// sec_redacted: the advertised SAFE display form. Hand-written and deliberately touches only
// `s.sec_label` -- never `s.sec_value` -- so it cannot leak regardless of whether the compiler's
// generic str()/show dispatch is trustworthy for this call site (measured: it is not, see header).
fn <T> sec_redacted(s: PrismSecret<T>) -> string
    "Secret({s.sec_label}: ***)"

// sec_to_json: the advertised SAFE wire form. Hand-written for the same reason as sec_redacted --
// this is the function a caller MUST use to put a secret-bearing value on a wire/patch payload,
// because the generic to_json() builtin is confirmed unsafe on this type once the caller's own
// file declares any struct of its own (the overwhelmingly common case). json_stringify() escapes
// the label exactly the way the compiler's own generated to_json escapes an ordinary string field.
fn <T> sec_to_json(s: PrismSecret<T>) -> string
    "\{\"sec_label\":" + json_stringify(s.sec_label) + ",\"sec_value\":\"***\"\}"

// sec_eq: constant-time for the common case (string secrets), honestly non-constant-time
// otherwise. See file header "EQUALITY THAT DOES NOT LEAK".
fn <T> sec_eq(a: PrismSecret<T>, b: PrismSecret<T>) -> bool
    let av = sec_unwrap(a)
    let bv = sec_unwrap(b)
    if type_of(av) == "string" and type_of(bv) == "string"
        return ct_eq(av, bv) == 1
    av == bv
